{"name":"American River","hex":"#626e71","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"American River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A river in California, United States that flows from the Sierra Nevadas to its confluence with the Sacramento River."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/American_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"American River","blocks":[{"text":"The American River is a 30-mile-long (50 km) river in California that runs from the Sierra Nevada mountain range to its confluence with the Sacramento River in downtown Sacramento. Via the Sacramento River, it is part of the San Francisco Bay watershed. This river is fed by the melting snowpack of the Sierra Nevada and its many headwaters and tributaries, including its North, Middle, and South Forks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/American_River_CA.jpg/330px-American_River_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_River","wikidata":"Q466699"},{"title":"American River (Washington)","blocks":[{"text":"The American River is a tributary of the Bumping River in Washington. It flows down the east side of the Cascade Range, through Wenatchee National Forest and the William O. Douglas Wilderness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/American_River_16075.JPG/330px-American_River_16075.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_River_(Washington)","wikidata":"Q3564307"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/California1838.jpg/500px-California1838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/California1838.jpg/1280px-California1838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/California1838.jpg/500px-California1838.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1838 map from Britannica 7th edition, when the river was called Buenaventura River.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACalifornia1838.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Guy_West_Bridge_on_the_American_River_.jpg/500px-Guy_West_Bridge_on_the_American_River_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Guy_West_Bridge_on_the_American_River_.jpg/500px-Guy_West_Bridge_on_the_American_River_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View of the American River from below the Guy West Bridge on the Sacramento State campus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuy_West_Bridge_on_the_American_River_.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/